{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1191 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "655c26cc5cbe5045ed7eb83366b80e62beebd8a4495ba86362841b7779f9f1eb" :edb/db-before-t 1190 :edb/db-after-t 1191 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534503 50 #inst "2026-10-07T18:34:27.761Z" 13194139534503 true] [17592186105489 1100 "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4$. Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f'(x)>0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/188a9a9a3d8e58ae595eacd0c7a3e567.png)" 13194139534503 false] [17592186105489 1100 "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where $f'(x)>0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/1a8208a75b7df62a0c692e08f74509ecf1462e3ab1f8dd7eae85a56272abde48.png)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186105489 1145 "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B$. Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0$.\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0$.\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & (- ∞, 1) & 1 & (1, 4) & 4 & (4, + ∞) \\\\ f & ↘ & min & ↗ & max & ↘ \\\\ f^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4)$." 13194139534503 false] [17592186105489 1145 "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,1) & 1 & (1,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186105491 1145 "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3x^{2} + 2x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3x^{2} + 2x - 8 < 0\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n3(x + 2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right) < 0\n$$\n\nand the graph of\n\n$$\ny=3(x+2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right)\n$$\n\nhas roots at $x=-2$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 4 3\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/75d6da8209635228de08be5e9bd44737.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nbetween the roots, so we conclude that $f'(x) < 0$ on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$" 13194139534503 false] [17592186105491 1145 "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2x-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n3(x + 2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right) < 0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=3(x+2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right)$ has roots at $x=-2$ and $x=\\dfrac 4 3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/14e4f6078b21d209082c548f7f5d064abeaa98ee737aff919d2f6efad7a317e8.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ between the roots, so we conclude that $f'(x) < 0$ on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186105494 1100 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{\\ln{2x}}{x}$ (plotted below) is strictly increasing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/2ccb7c95d06c78a1730f9ca46aad9b6f1370050c745f2c1fc9208275670c3649.png)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186105494 1100 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{\\ln{2x}}{x}$ (plotted below) is strictly increasing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/39ade411a2a5987fa7b7c2350ac26caf.png)" 13194139534503 false] [17592186105494 1145 "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) > 0$.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= ((\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot x - \\ln (2x) \\cdot 1)/(x^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\ln 2x}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\ln{2x}}{x^2} > 0\n$$\n\nThe denominator is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n1 - \\ln 2x > 0⟹\\ln 2x < 1\n$$\n\nSolving $\\ln{2x} = 1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\n2x = e\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\dfrac{1}{2}e\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a maximum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point.\n\nWe summarize this information in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & (0, \\frac{1}{2}e) & \\frac{1}{2}e & (\\frac{1}{2}e, ∞) \\\\ f & ↗ & max & ↘ \\\\ f^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\frac 1 2 e\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\n0 < x < \\dfrac 1 2 e\n$$" 13194139534503 false] [17592186105494 1145 "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) > 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\ln{2x}}{x^2} > 0.\n$$\n\nThe denominator is  always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x>0\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\ln{2x} = 1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\n2x = e\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\dfrac{1}{2}e.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a maximum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nWe summarize this information in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{2}e) & \\frac{1}{2}e & (\\frac{1}{2}e,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $\\left(0,\\frac 1 2 e\\right),$ which can also be written as $0 < x < \\dfrac 1 2 e.$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186105495 1100 "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/aa8ee7171576bc92bb9b6a6730223e2629bc0d6216cb54a7a8a9f51d97985d14.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f'(2.5) < f'(1) < f'(-1)$\n\n- $f'(-1) < f'(1) < f'(2.5)$\n\n- $f'(1) < f'(-1) < f'(2.5)$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186105495 1100 "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/57462b1da853695603be9e97d89cb7a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $f'(2.5) < f'(1) < f'(-1)$\n2. $f'(-1) < f'(1) < f'(2.5)$\n3. $f'(1) < f'(-1) < f'(2.5)$" 13194139534503 false] [17592186105495 1145 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so $f'(-1) > 0$.\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so $f'(1) = 0$.\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=2.5$ is negative, and so $f'(2.5) < 0$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is correct." 13194139534503 false] [17592186105495 1145 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so $f'(-1) > 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so $f'(1) = 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=2.5$ is negative, and so $f'(2.5) < 0.$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is correct." 13194139534503 true] [17592186105632 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292228 13194139534503 true] [17592186105632 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292235 13194139534503 true] [17592186105632 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292242 13194139534503 true] [17592186105632 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292333 13194139534503 true] [17592186105636 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292249 13194139534503 true] [17592186105636 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292256 13194139534503 true] [17592186105636 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292263 13194139534503 true] [17592186105636 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292284 13194139534503 true] [17592186105636 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292298 13194139534503 true] [17592186105637 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292291 13194139534503 true] [17592186105637 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292303 13194139534503 true] [17592186105637 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292308 13194139534503 true] [17592186105637 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292312 13194139534503 true] [17592186105637 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292319 13194139534503 true] [17592186105638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292270 13194139534503 true] [17592186105638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292277 13194139534503 true] [17592186105638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292326 13194139534503 true] [17592186105638 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292340 13194139534503 true] [17592186106015 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534503 true] [17592186106015 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/269b71c2ec5653b6577e904a43374a45a0830f671850c7157a8d1bbfb49308f3.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186106015 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-18227.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x = 0$ and $x = 2$. Given that $f^{′}(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f^{′}(x) < 0$." 13194139534503 false] [17592186106015 1145 "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(0,2).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186106288 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534503 true] [17592186106288 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/0a55a722c0b1f22b7a3d4c1f8d69869aa6a7dd571f85349049389c105b7190aa.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139534503 true] [17592186106288 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1359/Source/Images/q-48996.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y = f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139534503 false] [17592186106288 1145 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(0)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(1)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)<{