7592186292280 1034 "{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292280 1111 #uuid "9c205a26-5cd0-5dc1-9378-ea2978135209" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292281 1034 "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-1)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292281 1111 #uuid "762625ab-a2b2-5883-938b-0214796e9b8e" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292282 1034 "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(2)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292282 1111 #uuid "e4849d03-a641-5273-bab2-294f5e153518" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292283 1034 "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292283 1111 #uuid "1ba636d4-3511-5eaf-9ca0-daaedf356bb2" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292284 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292285 13194139534503 true] [17592186292284 1089 "q-21017" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292284 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534503 true] [17592186292284 1100 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=5-7x-4{x}^{2}$ is strictly decreasing." 13194139534503 true] [17592186292284 1113 #uuid "ebb3d0c2-683e-5c49-bf5b-f1008fff0100" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292284 1145 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-7-8x \\\\  & =-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-8(x+\\frac{7}{8})<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) & <0 \\\\ 0 & <8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) \\\\ 0 & <x+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{8} & <x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty ).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292286 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292287 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292288 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292289 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292290 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1041 "selection" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292288 13194139534503 true] [17592186292285 1110 #uuid "3563b7f6-7726-5e63-bb4d-9274479e90e9" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292286 1034 "x\\in (\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292286 1111 #uuid "d0b0d7b2-3ccc-5e5f-a751-92a8b183ffe4" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292287 1034 "x\\in (0,\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292287 1111 #uuid "3a7873af-440e-5a68-9a67-c3da6cd2c3bb" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292288 1034 "x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292288 1111 #uuid "e8418ace-cb19-54e4-be87-cd06a1a42793" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292289 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{7}{8})" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292289 1111 #uuid "cde47b04-4471-546f-b90b-d38396d71a07" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292290 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{8})" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292290 1111 #uuid "2b774eca-f0c2-58a1-af0f-cc7330fd2fca" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292291 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292292 13194139534503 true] [17592186292291 1089 "q-21019" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534503 true] [17592186292291 1100 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{x}$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." 13194139534503 true] [17592186292291 1113 #uuid "663fb6af-8507-5e62-bac7-090f5e3edc6e" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292291 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}>0.\n$$\n\nUsing that $x>0,$ the above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-1 & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x+1)(x-1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factors ${x}^{2}+1$ and $x+1$ are positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(1,\\infty ).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292293 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292294 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292295 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292296 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292297 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1041 "selection" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292297 13194139534503 true] [17592186292292 1110 #uuid "96e80dd6-8f4d-5974-b059-92a5d5f4d3f8" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292293 1034 "x\\in (0,1)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292293 1111 #uuid "c9041fc3-b3c2-513e-8267-87ec04aa78ff" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292294 1034 "x\\in (0,\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292294 1111 #uuid "245ee93e-2db1-52bc-a177-afceb060f288" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292295 1034 "x\\in (0,3)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292295 1111 #uuid "6ed137b1-88b7-57f9-b216-f1f7ede43161" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292296 1034 "x\\in (3,\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292296 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292296 1111 #uuid "fe7be3f4-2c4e-5ae1-be46-3488948fffcf" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292297 1034 "x\\in (1,\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292297 1111 #uuid "0e140852-b9dc-5e99-a4d3-4512ad29cead" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292298 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292299 13194139534503 true] [17592186292298 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292301 13194139534503 true] [17592186292298 1089 "q-343536" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534503 true] [17592186292298 1100 "Given that $g(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+1$ is defined for $x\\in (-8,8),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292298 1113 #uuid "1cf79dbe-8152-5ab4-8e50-e95e13f50f06" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292298 1145 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+12x \\\\  & =12x({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =12x{(x-1)}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x$, the sign of $12x{(x-1)}^{2}$ is determined by the sign of $12x$, so $12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0$ exactly when $12x<0.$ This yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (-8,8),$ we conclude that $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-8,0).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292299 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292300 13194139534503 true] [17592186292299 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534503 true] [17592186292299 1041 "field-1" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292299 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292300 13194139534503 true] [17592186292299 1110 #uuid "06e6dc4d-d27b-576e-92a6-5d1574f68606" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292300 1034 "-8" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292300 1111 #uuid "8dc9466a-ba33-599c-a377-df5c35fcb64a" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292302 13194139534503 true] [17592186292301 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534503 true] [17592186292301 1041 "field-2" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292301 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292302 13194139534503 true] [17592186292301 1110 #uuid "87d9b456-748b-5109-9440-4ede11ea60ab" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292302 1034 "0" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292302 1111 #uuid "8e75f2f3-ef44-594a-80ab-f93e4772707e" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292303 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292304 13194139534503 true] [17592186292303 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292306 13194139534503 true] [17592186292303 1089 "q-344091" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292303 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534503 true] [17592186292303 1100 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292303 1113 #uuid "80c35491-525d-5ce5-97f7-a307c606220b" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292303 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ has roots at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/26374f7e5c86c5585d384b3e65b4a1b3fb2f94172ed753cdb3fe7bdb0af0abb8.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ is positive when $x<\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x>3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x>3.\n$$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292304 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292305 13194139534503 true] [17592186292304 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534503 true] [17592186292304 1041 "field-1" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292304 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292305 13194139534503 true] [17592186292304 1110 #uuid "6e5e3246-f439-5276-879d-71aeb8663c62" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292305 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292305 1111 #uuid "1dc9d517-5024-5c53-b792-03b56e3d3695" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292307 13194139534503 true] [17592186292306 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534503 true] [17592186292306 1041 "field-2" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292306 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292307 13194139534503 true] [17592186292306 1110 #uuid "064aa38e-49f2-545e-bf28-9c26cf65d539" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292307 1034 "3" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292307 1111 #uuid "b8fb649d-4c24-507b-80b2-6474af2985ca" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292308 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292309 13194139534503 true] [17592186292308 1089 "q-344093" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292308 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534503 true] [17592186292308 1100 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x>$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292308 1113 #uuid "0476db05-5de2-5374-aca7-f167f44d351f" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292308 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-4)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y={x}^{2}(x-4)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/bfba80e21f4a458af6c4111191908a9d45d7d8c05f237db293cdad71a451d943.png)\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x,$ the product ${x}^{2}(x-4)$ is positive exactly when $x-4>0,$ that is, when $x>4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval \n\n$$\nx>4.\n$$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292310 13194139534503 true] [17592186292309 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292311 13194139534503 true] [17592186292309 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534503 true] [17592186292309 1041 "field-1" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292309 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292311 13194139534503 true] [17592186292309 1110 #uuid "1d174429-194a-5a07-844a-aa0fe83880d1" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292310 1034 "3" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292310 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292310 1111 #uuid "7ad66654-01c5-567f-9727-029b99131e7f" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292311 1034 "4" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292311 1111 #uuid "7ee1c080-ab3b-5e52-8b67-b160b6d4aec6" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292312 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292313 13194139534503 true] [17592186292312 1089 "q-48855" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292312 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534503 true] [17592186292312 1100 "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ is defined for $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." 13194139534503 true] [17592186292312 1113 #uuid "4bb806c1-d058-5c6e-9c3d-eb04de3f0030" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292312 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain is $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & <x<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <\\frac{x}{2}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that cosine is positive in the first and the fourth quadrant. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & <\\frac{x}{2}<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on the interval $-\\pi <x<0.$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292314 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292315 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292316 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292317 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292318 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1041 "selection" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292314 13194139534503 true] [17592186292313 1110 #uuid "60da7cc2-e305-5411-88c4-64a71faf4cd7" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292314 1034 "-\\pi <x<0" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292314 1111 #uuid "36fd1802-36cc-5158-95f1-7e974dd547e1" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292315 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<0" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292315 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292315 1111 #uuid "9cbf9759-de6f-55a0-86ef-a0eac25ea64f" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292316 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292316 1111 #uuid "d01aaa29-954c-534d-b66f-4d49291ad017" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292317 1034 "-\\pi <x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292317 1111 #uuid "92347dca-c256-5bae-99ae-abb6e552cc48" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292318 1034 "-2\\pi <x<\\pi " 13194139534503 true] [17592186292318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292318 1111 #uuid "562cd83c-5e19-521a-99d6-0a274ac06495" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292319 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292320 13194139534503 true] [17592186292319 1089 "q-48860" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292319 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534503 true] [17592186292319 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/61b89de34a6900d2ea39e5c2af141b3353e9ca790ac2748d0fc6d4d578127f93.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ (plotted above) is strictly increasing." 13194139534503 true] [17592186292319 1113 #uuid "680534fd-cbc6-5a80-9a00-31c6d6540a49" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292319 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x)$ using the product  rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a minimum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is negative to the left of the critical point and positive to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{e}) & \\frac{1}{e} & (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $(\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292321 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292322 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292323 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292324 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292325 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1041 "selection" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292325 13194139534503 true] [17592186292320 1110 #uuid "74ee2adc-bfa3-56a0-be8c-af463760c17a" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292321 1034 "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},e)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292321 1111 #uuid "fc711fe5-b65c-5a22-909f-1be8c43ad06a" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292322 1034 "x\\in (0,e)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292322 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292322 1111 #uuid "6e8e44c1-c248-5e64-9411-5bf5220c5424" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292323 1034 "x\\in (e,\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292323 1111 #uuid "432dc720-9e17-5ba8-9478-6ad706849a04" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292324 1034 "x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{e})" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292324 1111 #uuid "6b253c72-0412-543f-aaf9-c3c2acb9f20a" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292325 1034 "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty )" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292325 1111 #uuid "6fa7ebbf-a86c-5ebf-b55c-794fc32e3ab3" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292326 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292327 13194139534503 true] [17592186292326 1089 "q-49006" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292326 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534503 true] [17592186292326 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/ae52c0cfa5238c68574fdc3eff16618b84ce2dfaa5846df5300bd5602b8628f5.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292326 1113 #uuid "e31c47ec-52ee-5c63-a42a-61e28cfe1717" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292326 1145 "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-2)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-1)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(1)=0.$\n\nTherefore , ${f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292328 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292329 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292330 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292331 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292332 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1041 "selection" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1104 #edb/ref 17592186292330 13194139534503 true] [17592186292327 1110 #uuid "ccd41fc8-90f1-5385-b65a-e2db6923c18a" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292328 1034 "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(-1)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292328 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292328 1111 #uuid "0c6172c5-087b-518a-bff2-2ae51e8d4ec9" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292329 1034 "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-2)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292329 1111 #uuid "5287580a-9338-5293-b8cb-9a9446a9eca6" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292330 1034 "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292330 1111 #uuid "64f7ceca-6673-54dc-a9a6-800ca55235a1" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292331 1034 "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292331 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292331 1111 #uuid "dc16cb13-d6fd-53eb-9556-6c5b2d400168" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292332 1034 "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-2)" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534503 true] [17592186292332 1111 #uuid "938e2683-9878-5c90-8a72-51090e228074" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292333 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292334 13194139534503 true] [17592186292333 1089 "q-73443" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292333 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534503 true] [17592186292333 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/e0adac4b5e6a66f6c6a38af96f2e55c05ec98a87857af736e40d22b31fdf259b.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292333 1113 #uuid "661e6b85-8613-5f9a-9927-c76d05b988ae" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292333 1145 "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,3).$" 13194139534503 true] [17592186292334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292335 13194139534503 true] [17592186292334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292336 13194139534503 true] [17592186292334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292337 13194139534503 true] [17592186292334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186292338 13194139534503 true] [17592186292334 1000 #edb/ref 1759218